# Machine Code vs Byte Code

| Machine Code                                               | Byte Code                                                          |
| ---------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Machine code is directly understood by the computer’s CPU. | Byte code is an intermediate code understood by a virtual machine. |
| It runs directly on hardware.                              | It needs a virtual machine like JVM to run.                        |
| It is very fast.                                           | It is slower than machine code.                                    |
| It is machine-dependent.                                   | It is platform-independent.                                        |
| Generated after final compilation.                         | Generated before machine code.                                     |
| Example: C language compiled output.                       | Example: Java bytecode (`.class` files).                           |
 In Easy Words

* **Machine Code** = Final language that computer hardware understands directly.
* **Byte Code** = Middle-level code that first goes to a virtual machine, then runs on the computer.
